1
00:00:00,328 --> 00:00:06,439
A studio development team decides whether to
chase a book, a comic, or a format.

2
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:08,497
Those calls are worth millions,

3
00:00:08,557 --> 00:00:13,434
and the research behind them sits in trade
articles and someone's memory.

4
00:00:13,494 --> 00:00:17,549
BLACKBOOK researches an intellectual property,
makes the call, records why,

5
00:00:17,609 --> 00:00:21,664
and watches for the moment that call stops
being right.

6
00:00:22,147 --> 00:00:25,909
Every decision the system has ever made is
here.

7
00:00:25,969 --> 00:00:33,131
Each one carries a recommendation — pursue,
watch, or pass — a score out of a hundred,

8
00:00:33,191 --> 00:00:37,803
and the four components underneath it: market
opportunity, rights confidence, competition,

9
00:00:37,863 --> 00:00:39,078
and production feasibility.

10
00:00:39,554 --> 00:00:43,333
Gemini scores those four components and
explains its reasoning.

11
00:00:43,393 --> 00:00:45,893
It never picks the final number.

12
00:00:45,953 --> 00:00:53,146
A fixed weighted function in our own code
turns those components into the score and the recommendation.

13
00:00:53,206 --> 00:00:55,706
The model explains; the system decides.

14
00:00:55,766 --> 00:00:59,119
The same inputs always produce the same
decision.

15
00:00:59,605 --> 00:01:02,254
Behind every decision is an evidence graph.

16
00:01:02,314 --> 00:01:08,833
Each claim is a node with a typed relationship
to another, not a paragraph of summary text.

17
00:01:09,324 --> 00:01:15,235
Open any node and you get the source, when it
was published, when we observed it,

18
00:01:15,295 --> 00:01:19,341
a confidence level, and the exact sentence the
claim came from.

19
00:01:19,401 --> 00:01:21,581
Nothing in a decision is unattributed.

20
00:01:22,062 --> 00:01:26,826
The stress test asks what would have to change
for this call to flip.

21
00:01:26,886 --> 00:01:30,962
Every scenario runs through the same scoring
function as the real decision,

22
00:01:31,022 --> 00:01:34,408
so these numbers are exactly what the engine
would produce.

23
00:01:34,468 --> 00:01:39,923
The smallest change that moves this off pursue
is a thirty point drop in market opportunity.

24
00:01:40,398 --> 00:01:44,035
When nothing can flip a recommendation, the
system says so.

25
00:01:44,095 --> 00:01:49,951
It reports how much margin is left instead of
inventing a change that does not exist.

26
00:01:50,425 --> 00:01:52,532
Decisions do not stay correct.

27
00:01:52,592 --> 00:01:57,302
A Parallel monitor watches the web for events
touching this property.

28
00:01:57,362 --> 00:02:03,372
Each event carries its own provenance, is
de-duplicated so one story never fires twice,

29
00:02:03,432 --> 00:02:06,841
and is tested for materiality before anything
changes.

30
00:02:07,319 --> 00:02:10,847
The result is a decision that keeps a memory.

31
00:02:10,907 --> 00:02:16,030
This property went from pursue, to watch when
a competing adaptation was announced,

32
00:02:16,090 --> 00:02:18,821
to pass when the rights were sold.

33
00:02:18,881 --> 00:02:22,011
Three linked revisions, every one evidenced
and explained.
